WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning comedian

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:06.339
and actor you know from shows
like "Extras" and "The Office."

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.674
The third and final season
of his show

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:10.777
"After Life"
is streaming now on Netflix.

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.345
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:16.382
-[ Air horn blares ]
-Aah!

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:20.186
-[ Laughing ]
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.822
-Prat!
-[ Laughing ]

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:25.991
Maybe life
is still worth living!

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:28.027
Oh, my God.
-That's not funny, actually.

00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:30.296
-Wow. I disagree.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.932
-Please welcome back to the
show our friend Ricky Gervais!

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:34.667
Hello, Ricky!

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:36.469
-Hello.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.639
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:42.441
-Now, Ricky, I want to ask
first about the clip.

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:46.212
Is that a functional air horn?

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:50.149
-It was real,
and that was the first take,

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.651
and we nailed it,

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:56.189
but I did it again about another
10 times for my own amusement.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.325
So the actor
got more and more injured,

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:01.227
and that's obviously
a real laugh

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:05.197
because someone falling over
and hurting themselves is funny.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:08.334
That is comedy to me.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.870
-The last time
you were here in studio,

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.639
we talked about the first season
of this show, "After Life,"

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:16.242
and I complimented you
on what a wonderful job

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.944
you did casting out this show.

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:20.680
It is such a -- Even though
you were torturing them

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.415
and making them fall down
and hurt themselves,

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:25.718
this is really a wonderful group
of people that you put together.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.621
-Yeah. I mean, the good thing
about this is,

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.189
once you've been around
for a while,

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:32.124
I can cast before I write it.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:36.729
So when you first write
something, you send it off,

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.732
you get it greenlit,
and then you have to cast.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:41.467
You're trying to work out
who to do it.

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.269
And the second season
is always the best,

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.003
because you know
who you're writing for.

00:01:45.003 --> 00:01:46.539
You know the physicality.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.941
You know what they're good at,
whether they can ad-lib or not.

00:01:48.941 --> 00:01:50.743
With this,
as I thought of a character,

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:54.013
I knew who could play it,
and I was calling them.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:57.350
I said, "Do you want to be -- "
In fact, when I called Kerry,

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:01.454
who plays my late wife, I said,
"Oh, I've got an idea.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:02.922
You play my dead wife."

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:05.325
And she went,
"What, you mean I'm a corpse?"

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.893
I went, "No, no, no.
No, you're not a corpse."

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:11.797
...some weird '60s sitcom.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:15.135
But, yeah, it makes it --
it's nearly cheating,

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.836
because it makes it
so much easier,

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.371
and they're there from the
beginning, and you workshop it,

00:02:19.371 --> 00:02:23.042
so it all looks so real.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:26.412
The chemistry's good,
and it looks so natural.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.515
-So you usually --
Historically,

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:32.284
you have done two seasons of
your shows and then a special.

00:02:32.284 --> 00:02:35.621
This is a full third season
on Netflix.

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:38.591
I'm wondering, what was it about
this story that you wanted

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.794
to go a little bit further,
as far as number of episodes?

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.564
-It's slightly different.
It's not a traditional sitcom.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:48.801
So I suppose it had
a more dramatic pace to it,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:52.238
so there was a story to tell.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.506
The themes are bigger --

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:57.910
life, death, you know,
mortality, grieving.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.245
They were bigger.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.747
I built up such an ensemble
of characters.

00:03:01.747 --> 00:03:03.149
It was like a Springfield.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.685
So I had a lot of
characters to sort of --

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:09.822
And it's only about an hour and
a half longer than those things.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.124
It's only 18 half-hours,

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:14.159
which is not even
an American season.

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:16.395
That's not even a whole season.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:19.098
They're going, "He did
18 episodes. What a trouper.

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:22.768
What a --
He should be given an award."

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:25.204
You know, so --
But yeah, those are the reasons.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.106
And I loved it.
I absolutely loved it.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:30.343
But it's still best
to go out on top.

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:33.679
It seems mad -- It seems mad
to end it now at its peak,

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:37.583
but I think ending it now
will mean it will last

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.185
for 10 years,
if you know what i mean.

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:41.587
It's always there.
It's a finite story.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:45.091
And I just think --
It'll outlive me, definitely.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.659
It'll definitely outlive me.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.994
-This is a show,
like you mentioned --

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:51.297
It's about grieving.
It's about death.

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:52.965
Was that something
you were thinking about,

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.801
ruminating about
before you had this idea?

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:57.336
-No. It was really
a jumping-off point.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:04:01.273
The idea was a guy
who'd lost everything

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:02.575
and didn't care about living.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.545
He was gonna kill himself,
but the dog was hungry.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.145
And he treated it
like a superpower,

00:04:07.145 --> 00:04:08.514
like he could do anything

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:10.983
and then he could always
fall back on suicide.

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:13.886
And that was
the jumping-off bit.

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:16.322
But then it became
a sort of study in grief,

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.991
because after the first series
went out,

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:22.695
so many people came up to me
and they said,

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.563
"I lost my brother,"
or, "I lost my wife,"

00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:27.633
or, "I lost my mother two weeks
before I watched,"

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:31.070
and you suddenly realize
that everyone's grieving.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.008
People like seeing a bit
of themselves on the TV,

00:04:35.008 --> 00:04:37.009
even if it's the bad stuff.

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:40.446
Especially if it's the bit that
people don't usually see on TV,

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.616
if it's a taboo subject
and not dealt with,

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.884
particularly if they see
the funny side of it,

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:47.553
because that's what comedy
is for,

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.455
for getting us over bad stuff.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.690
And so I realized that people
really identified

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:52.958
with that side of it,

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:55.594
and I wanted to treat it
with respect,

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:59.065
because that's why he doesn't
just snap out of it, you know?

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.700
That's why it's a long struggle.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.202
And it is about him

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:06.305
going through
the seven stages of grief.

00:05:06.305 --> 00:05:08.040
It just asked the big question,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.444
"If you lose everything,
is life still worth living?"

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:14.113
Without spoilers.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:17.449
You need to find something
to make it worthwhile,

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:20.052
and that's what
he's looking for.

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:21.320
-When you're writing,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.656
what part is most important
for you to tackle first?

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.758
Making it funny, which it is,

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.327
or also making it
sort of nail the landing

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:29.895
as far as the emotional arc?

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:32.765
Which one do you focus on more?

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:37.036
-Is it real? Is it honest?
Is it original?

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:40.272
Have I seen it on telly before?
Is it frightening?

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:41.540
All those things.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.475
And when you think
of a great idea,

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:44.977
"Can I make it funny?"

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:48.781
And the answer is usually yes.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:52.017
My stand-up is --
I talk about taboo subjects

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:54.387
because I want to take
the audience

00:05:54.387 --> 00:05:56.088
to a place
it hasn't been before,

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:57.590
and I can feel it in the room.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.593
When I bring up a contentious
subject, I can feel the tension.

00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:04.663
It's like I take them by
the hand through a scary forest

00:06:04.663 --> 00:06:06.698
and out the other side
and everything's alright.

00:06:06.698 --> 00:06:08.934
Because there's nothing
you shouldn't joke about.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:11.136
It depends on the joke.

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:13.806
And I've always thought that,
and I've always been fascinated

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:16.375
with that side of things,
because it's part of life.

00:06:16.375 --> 00:06:21.280
We're all gonna die,
so let's joke about it.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:22.448
Let's have a laugh.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.050
-I know you've been doing
some stand-up again.

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.884
I want to talk about
your upcoming special.

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:28.721
We'll be right back with more
from Ricky Gervais.

